{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["The reason for <b>בְּנִכְלֵיהֶם</b> (\"with their wiles\") is as I explained in my comment on Numbers 25:1 — that the elders of Midian devised this evil scheme, as it says: \"And Moab said to the elders of Midian\" (Numbers 22:4), for with them they took counsel at the outset. They gave this advice to Moab, that their daughters should prostitute themselves with them, and they would attach them to Baal Peor to draw them away from HaShem — and if not, they would not be mentioned there. Furthermore, they sent them their king's daughter to prostitute herself with them, and this is the reason for \"and concerning the matter of Cozbi, daughter of the prince of Midian, their sister\" — for had this been done without the knowledge of the kingdom's counselors, what would a royal princess of Midian be seeking in Shittim, in the steppes of Moab, coming into the camp of another people? For Israel was in the steppes of Moab at Shittim, and from where would this Midianite woman come there? Rather, she was exceedingly beautiful, and the elders of Midian sent her there, for they said: \"Through a beautiful woman's appearance many have been corrupted\" (Yevamot 63a). It is also likely that Balaam was in this counsel as well, for on his return from the land of Moab he passed through Midian since his route lay that way, and he was among the kingdom's counselors. Perhaps he even lingered there to find out what would happen to them; therefore Israel found him in Midian and killed him there (Numbers 31:8). And the meaning of \"And Balaam arose, went, and returned to his place\" (Numbers 24:25) would be that he set out to return to his land. So too it appears, for Israel would not kill a prophet save by authority of the Torah; but when they were told, \"For they are adversaries to you in their wiles, with which they have dealt treacherously with you,\" and so forth — behold, all who act treacherously are liable to death, and therefore they also put Balaam to death, for they knew that he was the mastermind of this evil counsel. Now the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded them to take vengeance upon them, but concerning the Moabites He had already warned: \"Do not harass Moab\" (Deuteronomy 2:9), for Israel at first came to the border of Edom and He warned them concerning them, \"Take good heed\" (ibid. v. 4), and afterward they came to the border of Moab and He said to him, \"Do not harass Moab,\" and afterward He said, \"You are to pass over the border of Moab, Ar, and you will come near opposite the children of Ammon; do not besiege them\" (ibid. vv. 18–19), and He said, \"Rise up, take your journey, and cross the Arnon; see, I have given Sihon into your hand\" (ibid. v. 24). And after all this, Balak saw all that Israel had done to the Amorite — the two Amorite kings, Sihon and Og — and this entire matter came to pass. If so, the warning \"Do not harass Moab\" precedes the commandment of \"Harass the Midianites.\" I am puzzled by the language of the Aggadah that they stated in Baba Kamma (38a): \"'Do not harass Moab' — did it enter Moses' mind to wage war without permission? Rather, Moses himself drew an inference of kal va-chomer: if concerning the Midianites, who came only to assist Moab, the Torah said, 'Harass the Midianites and strike them,' then the Moabites themselves, all the more so! The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: Not as what entered your mind — two fledglings I have to bring forth from them: Ruth the Moabitess and Naamah the Ammonitess.\" Now, before the kal va-chomer He had warned Moses concerning the Moabites, and furthermore, if it was on account of the kal va-chomer, why was there need to warn concerning the children of Ammon? Perhaps they were not precise in the language of the Aggadah in this, but the intent is to say that it was revealed before Him, blessed be He, that when He would command him \"Harass the Midianites,\" Moses would have a kal va-chomer to wage war against Moab without other authorization, so He prefaced by warning him against this. And since He warned concerning Moab, He also warned concerning the children of Ammon, lest Moses think that His warning against Moab alone leaves room for the children of Ammon, for they too repaid them evil and did not greet them with bread and water. And another midrash: because the Moabites acted out of fear, for they were afraid Israel would plunder them, but the Midianites were incensed over a quarrel that was not theirs, as Rashi wrote in the portion of Masei (Rashi on Numbers 31:2). And by way of the plain sense further: the land of Ammon and Moab, HaShem gave it to the children of Lot as an inheritance on account of their father Lot, who served the righteous man and went into exile with him to that land, and therefore He did not permit them to do them harm in their land. But because of their sin — that they distanced Israel and did not draw them near — He punished them by distancing them from His people, and commanded: \"An Ammonite and a Moabite shall not enter the assembly of YHWH\" (Deuteronomy 23:4) — measure for measure."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:09:02.047763275Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}